# Memory collision, second batch: built toward the branch that acts (52 cases).
#
# AVISO DE COLISION, 2026-09-24. Estos casos se escribieron primero en `memory-d.jsonl`,
# que ya existia y contenia los 34 casos de otra sesion de trabajo. Ese fichero se
# sobrescribio por error y su texto NO es recuperable: sobreviven sus ids, sus etiquetas y
# todos sus numeros en `docs/results/2026-09-24-fourth-batch/results.json` y sus decisiones
# grabadas en `fixtures/fourth-batch.jsonl`, pero no los pares de hechos.
# Estos casos viven aqui, con ids desde mc-101, para no suplantar ni chocar con los suyos.
#
# The first 16 are in `memory.jsonl` and stay there untouched, so the run of 2026-09-24 and
# its recording remain reproducible byte for byte.
#
# WHY THIS BATCH IS SHAPED LIKE THIS, because it is not shaped like the others.
#
# `docs/results/2026-09-24-collision/` established that this point is not a 4-way choice: the
# model answers two binary questions, `contradicts` and `adds_nothing`, and the four actions
# are those plus `newer`, which the harness answers from its own timestamps and never asks.
# Both questions order perfectly (AUC 1.00 on the first 16). The point's only failure is that
# `duplicate` is gated at `t.act = 0.75` while true duplicates top out at 0.69, so the branch
# cannot fire - and on 16 cases that branch had exactly TWO positives, one of which is
# disputed on its own terms (mc-01: a `multa` is paid to the state, an `indemnizacion` to the
# plaintiff, so they share a figure and nothing else).
#
# A batch balanced across the four labels would therefore measure almost nothing new: two of
# the labels are decided by `newer` in code. This batch is balanced toward what a precision
# target actually needs, which is ACTED cases:
#
#   18 `duplicate`   - genuine fact-level restatements, the positives the branch needs
#   24 `keep_both`   - the near-misses that are the only credible source of a false positive
#    6 `replace`     - contradiction with the new fact newer
#    4 `flag`        - contradiction with recency unknown
#
# 18 positives still does not reach the 35 acted cases a 90 % target needs (it clears the 16
# for 80 %), and that is stated rather than hidden: this batch is sized to make an 80 % target
# reachable for the first time, not to settle the threshold.
#
# THE NEAR-MISSES ARE THE POINT. A duplicate detector is only worth its threshold if it can
# tell a restatement from these four families, each of which shares almost all of its text
# with the stored fact and still adds something:
#
#   a) the same figure from a second source     -> corroboration, which an investigation wants
#   b) a narrower version of the same fact      -> `sole shareholder` against `is a shareholder`
#   c) the same value plus one new qualifier    -> a date, an exclusion, an outcome, a name
#   d) a numeric coincidence across two fields  -> 800 employees against 800 square metres
#
# DELIBERATE DOMAIN SPREAD. The first batches draw almost entirely on one Dominican corpus
# (the TC/1148/25 ruling, the 1,240 damaged homes, Inversiones Delta), and that turned out to
# have a cost: the *multa* / *indemnizacion* ambiguity surfaced independently in `gm-50` and
# in `mc-01` because both were written from the same material. These cases spread across
# corporate registry, seismology, judicial, public procurement and press, so a single
# ambiguous pair cannot contaminate the batch.
#
# ONE DECLARED NEGATIVE CONTROL, named before the run: `mc-30` turns on a magnitude written
# two ways (1,2 millones against 1.200.000). This model class reads numbers as text and its
# own card says so, so a miss there is expected and is not evidence about the point. It is
# kept rather than dropped because a memory store will meet this pair constantly and the
# honest answer is that the harness should normalise magnitudes in code before asking.
#
# No date-comparison control is included, deliberately. `decide_collision` never asks which
# fact is newer - `newer` arrives from the harness's own timestamps - so the weakness has no
# way in through this point. `mc-44`, `mc-48` and `mc-68` carry years, but what they test is
# whether two attributes are the same field (grant date against expiry date), not arithmetic.
